WEBVTT

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:05.440
Good to have you on SpaceX Daily, Episode 11, coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.320
It's June 22, 2026.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.080
Plenty moving across Starship, Falcon, and Starlink today.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:18.760
A 2022 Geomagnetic Storm that destroyed 40 Starlink satellites is now informing how the

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.920
constellation hardens against atmospheric drag as launch rates climb.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:29.280
In February, 2022, a Geomagnetic Storm so minor it barely rated a mention,

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:35.520
thickened the upper atmosphere just enough to drag 40 of SpaceX freshly launched Starlink

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.480
satellites back to a fiery death.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.540
The event showed how even modest solar activity can raise thermosphere

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:48.400
density enough to increase drag on satellites at 200 to 300 kilometers altitude.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.640
Starlink satellites were still in their initial low-parking orbits when the storm hit,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.460
leaving insufficient margin for orbit raising burns.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:04.020
Operators have since added automated drag compensation logic and raised initial insertion

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:10.660
altitudes on later missions. Last covered on Episode 10, Starlink's direct-to-sell and subscriber

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:16.780
growth questions now gain another data point on how the constellation maintains plain spacing

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:23.440
when solar activity spikes. The US Space Force is shifting additional protected communications

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.160
traffic onto Starshield platforms after early operational demonstrations.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.160
Starshield uses the same flat panel phased array architecture as commercial Starlink,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.520
but adds radiation, hardened processors, and encrypted cross-links.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:44.800
The move expands the military user base beyond initial test units and ties into broader

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:51.440
resilient SAT-COM architecture goals. Reports indicate the transition follow successful early

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.920
demonstrations that validated the platform under operational conditions. Space daily noted that

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.340
Starshield maintains the core antenna design while incorporating the specialized components required

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:09.120
for military environments. The shift aligns with Space Force objectives for diversified

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:16.280
and survivable communications pathways. Observers on R Space X noted the Roman Space Telescopes

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.980
rival as the first major science payload confirmed for Falcon Heavy this summer.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:27.640
Commenters highlighted the 70-day processing window and asked whether any last-minute avionics

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:34.440
updates would affect the August target. Separate threads discussed how the 2022 Geomagnetic Storm

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.740
data is being folded into current Starlink software updates for autonomous drag avoidance.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:44.600
A few users shared photos of the arriving telescope container train at Kennedy.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:52.300
The $4.3 billion Observatory completed shipment to Kennedy Space Center and begins roughly 70 days of

00:02:52.300 --> 00:03:04.420
pre-launch processing. It is manifested on a Falcon Heavy no earlier than August 30th using the same

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:10.960
The telescope's wide field infrared instrument will require precise orbit insertion to reach its

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:18.200
planned Sun-Earth L2 Halo orbit. A Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg carrying 24 satellites

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:24.540
and completed its 34th booster landing on the drone ship. The mission continues the West Coast

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.320
Starlink cadence while the booster fleet accumulates additional flight cycles toward

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:36.920
life limit data. Last covered on episode 10 Falcon Reuse Cycle tracking now adds one more data point on

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:43.180
the path to understanding annual launch rate ceilings. Worth keeping an eye on SpaceX shares are

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:49.360
again under pressure as US markets reopen with reports citing continued heavy capital needs for

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:56.260
Starship and Starlink expansion even after the recent IPO. The stock's post-listing volatility

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:02.380
reflects investor debate over the pace at which launch and constellation spending will translate into

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:08.440
free cash flow. Any near-term improvement would require either faster Starlink subscriber growth

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:15.220
or visible progress on cost per launch metrics. Google news summaries indicated that analysts are

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:21.420
watching capital expenditure timelines closely following the June 12 listing. The counterpoint segment

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:27.280
highlighted ongoing investor focus on when Starship and Starlink outlays begin to yield measurable

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:33.640
returns. Market observers noted that subscriber metrics and per-flight cost trends remain the primary

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:39.980
variables under discussion. On the AI front, no new developments reported today on the Orbital

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:46.480
Data Center or XAI Compute Integration fronts. While watchers continue to track Starlink's direct

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:53.740
to sell backhaul potential and colossus cluster utilization. From an engineering standpoint, the 2022

00:04:53.740 --> 00:05:00.100
Geomagnetic Storm episode illustrates how thermosphereic density responds to solar input on time

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:06.980
scales of hours. Even a modest increase in extreme ultraviolet flux heats the upper atmosphere,

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.940
raising neutral density at 200 kilometers by tens of percent, and therefore increasing aerodynamic

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:20.800
drag on satellites whose ballistic coefficients are already low. Starlink's first generation satellites

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.900
still climbing through that altitude band after deployment, lacked sufficient propellant margin or

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:33.160
autonomous compensation to counteract the sudden torque. Subsequent design iterations added both higher

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:39.740
insertion orbits and onboard density estimation algorithms that command earlier orbit raising burns

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:46.760
when solar indices exceed thresholds. The same physics now drive decisions on how many spare satellites

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:53.320
must ride in each plane and how much margin the next generation laser-linked birds will carry for station

00:05:53.320 --> 00:06:02.380
keeping during solar maximum peaks and a quick market note. Sarge account is at $185 up 1.8% versus

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:09.200
the previous close. Before we go, the Roman space telescopes 70-day processing window at Kennedy

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.640
will be the next visible marker on whether the August 30th Falcon Heavy target holds.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:23.500
And that's a wrap on today's SpaceX developments. SPCX is trading at $185 up 1.8%.

